>>> Uncle George said, referring to the "Hardware Compatibility List"
>>> posted at http://www.digital.com/semiconductor/alpha/alpha-hcl.htm:
>
> that list does not make sense to me. the scsi drives should all be
> compatable, so should the scsi controllers, although less likely to be.
> The video cards the same - there shouldn't be any hardware incompatabilities
> there either, but apparently the list says otherwise. Ide drives.
> 
> I guess i dont understand the issues, for i think there shouldn't be any.

>>> and then Stephen Zedalis said:
> 
> What about cards/devices that have an intel based bios rom or require a
> Windoze driver.  Not necessarily gonna run Alpha machine code.

This is indeed the crucial issue; the fact that there are SCSI/IDE HDDs/CDs
listed is just a matter of completeness; when they say those have been
verified, it doesn't necessarily mean that others have failed the tests.
And in the case of the video cards, it's more of a list of which vendors of
expensive/high-performance cards have drivers that will run on the Alphas,
and which may benefit the most from the additional Alpha horsepower.

But back to the main issue...

Both video and SCSI controllers have BIOS on them, and the latest
revisions of AlphaBIOS attempt to init the cards using an x86 emulator to
run that BIOS code. In fact, its the *same* emulator that is part of MILO
that we use to init the VGAs via their BIOS code.

Now, the BIOS emulation is *NOT* just a matter of handling the x86
instructions, which is really the easy part. The emulator must also be able
to handle bus memory and IO space accesses; in the case of PCI bus, this
also includes Config space. Now, on the Alphas, access to the latter is done
in a completely different way than on INTEL-based PCs, so the emulator must
detect what's being attempted by the INTEL code, and perform the appropriate
equivalent on the Alpha.

It's complicated by the fact that there are *two* hardware "mechanisms" for
PCI Config space access defined in the spec, whose sequences of INTEL code
are sufficiently different to require different detection methods.

In the case of the Matrox Millennium, the BIOS code actually uses *BOTH*
mechanisms; the BIOS emulator only supports *ONE*. Fortunately, in this case,
none of the unsupported mechanism operations seem to be critical, and, in most
instances, don't cause additional problems (but I have stories... ).

The above is just one example, but I think you can see what can happen.
Be glad that there *is* such a list; it gives those people who don't wish
to "push the envelope" a sense of security that what they buy will work.

--Jay++

