From: Bryan B. Thompson <bryan@cog-tech.com>
Subject: casting int to / from pointer on alpha

Hello,

I am porting an application with some X11 interface code that was originally
ported years back from SunView, so its definately got a "legacy".  There are
some instances in the code where XtCallbackProc instances are used and the
old code assumed that one of the parameters, properly a pointer, could swallow
an 'int' and not burp.  Well, its burping.

I do not understand how the alpha handles explicit (or implicit) int/void*
casts.  Is it doing sign extension?  Preserving the lower 32 bits?

What I would like to find is an appropriate incantation for casting an int
to/from a pointer that will work on the alpha, and elsewhere as well.  Can
this be done in general, or must I make sure that the 'int' in question is
typed as a 'long' on the alpha so that it happily can co-exist in the space
of a pointer.

This is all C programming, of course.  I'm sorry if this is too off target
for this list, but I though some people out there could answer this one from
experience and the answer seems of general interest in porting (bad) code to
the alpha.

Thanks,

-- bryan thompson
